  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Refining Attendance Marking**\n\nI\'ve been examining the `LibraryRepository.kt` file and noticed a critical limitation in the `markAttendance` function. Currently, attendance is only being recorded for today, which is not ideal. To address this, I\'m now focusing on modifying the method to accept a date parameter, enabling flexible attendance marking across different dates, to support feature needs.\n\n\n**Analyzing Date Logic**\n\nI\'m now fully focused on the attendance status and date logic within the system. My key concern is aligning the UI with the required features. I\'ve pinpointed that the \"Late\" and \"Leave\" statuses are redundant, so I\'m updating the `AttendanceStatus` enum to reflect only \"Present\" and \"Absent\" values. Simultaneously, the UI will be updated to remove redundant status indicators and streamline the user interface with dedicated \"Present\" and \"Absent\" buttons, while also including a functional date selector.\n\n\n**Implementing Date Selector**\n\nI\'m now integrating a date selection component. I\'m prioritizing intuitive navigation, so I\'m implementing quick chips for common dates like \"Today\" and \"Yesterday.\" A Material 3 DatePickerDialog will be used for selecting any past date. The UI will prominently display the selected date, along with a \"Today,\" \"Yesterday,\" or \"X Days Ago\" badge and should indicate whether records are read-only or editable for past dates. Crucially, I\'m ensuring that attendance data dynamically updates based on the selected date!\n\n**Defining Editability Rules**\n\nNow I\'m designing the date-based editability rules. My approach allows marking or editing attendance for \"Today,\" \"Yesterday,\" and \"the day before yesterday.\" Attendance records older than two days will be locked and read-only.\n\n\n**Establishing Editability Logic**\n\nNow I\'m tackling the rules for date editability. Future dates remain uneditable; attendance can\'t be marked ahead of time. When within the allowed 2-day window, action buttons are clickable and an \"Editable\" badge appears. For older dates, everything becomes read-only: status badges display the records, disabled or hidden buttons indicate no modifications are possible, and a clear banner warns about locked records.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 3
      nanos: 455699258
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
