  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Library Registrations**\n\nI\'m currently focused on the registration process for new libraries, specifically how the system updates various data structures when a new library is registered using `registerFullLibrary` or `registerOwner`. I\'ve observed that several state variables are updated during this process. My next step involves scrutinizing how the system interacts with students, attendance, payments, expenses, and registration requests.\n\n\n**Addressing Data Persistence Issues**\n\nI\'ve uncovered some critical issues regarding data persistence during library registration and login. The system currently retains hardcoded mock data even for new registrations, which is not what we want. Furthermore, existing account data is not being restored upon login, whether through password or OTP, meaning saved library profiles, student information, and financial records are lost. The login screen itself pre-populates user data, leading to a confusing initial experience. I\'m now clarifying the requirement: a completely fresh, clean state is essential for new library registrations, meaning no predefined or mock data.\n\n\n**Defining Fresh State Parameters**\n\nNow I\'m focusing on the specifics of a \"fresh state\" for new library registrations. It means absolutely zero pre-existing data: no students, attendance records, payments, expenses, or registration requests. All seats must be marked as available. The audit log should reflect the fresh registration. The new library gets a fresh branch for its primary campus, reflecting an initial clean slate for all associated data structures.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 2
      nanos: 660585453
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
